Start each loaded game from a clean slate when the core stays loaded

libblastem.c keeps two pieces of per-game state in file statics that nothing
resets between games:

- `started`, set by the first retro_run so later frames RESUME the system
  rather than start it. A second retro_load_game in the same process gets a
  freshly allocated system that was never started, and retro_run resumes it:
  the 68000 never reads its reset vector. Measured with Tools/md-bench reload
  (Sonic 1, 900 frames): 0 of 900 frames identical to a first load, the very
  first one at the wrong width.
- `fb`, the picture buffer, and `last_fb`, the interlace field it last showed.
  The first frame of the next game was drawn over the previous game's last
  picture (1 frame of 900 differed with `started` alone reset).

RetroArch unloads the core's library between games, which resets every static,
so upstream never meets this. Retro Pal links the core into the app and loads
game after game into it.

With this patch, Tools/md-bench reload: every frame of a second load identical
to the first, through retro_unload_game, retro_deinit and retro_init as the
app does it.

--- a/libblastem.c
+++ b/libblastem.c
@@ -263,9 +263,14 @@
 
 /* Loads a game. */
 static system_type stype;
+static void reset_frame_output(void);
 RETRO_API bool retro_load_game(const struct retro_game_info *game)
 {
 	serialize_size_cache = 0;
+	//a frontend that keeps the core loaded between games must not resume a
+	//system that was never started, nor show the last game's picture
+	started = 0;
+	reset_frame_output();
 	stype = SYSTEM_UNKNOWN;
 	if (game->data) {
 		if (game->path) {
@@ -392,6 +397,11 @@
 
 static uint32_t fb[LINEBUF_SIZE * 294 * 2];
 static uint8_t last_fb;
+static void reset_frame_output(void)
+{
+	memset(fb, 0, sizeof(fb));
+	last_fb = 0;
+}
 uint32_t *render_get_framebuffer(uint8_t which, int *pitch)
 {
 	*pitch = LINEBUF_SIZE * sizeof(uint32_t);
